&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Which SEO tools do you actually need right now?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://53.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hub/53/hubfs/image8-Jul-27-2026-07-17-32-2545-AM.png?width=650&amp;amp;height=374&amp;amp;name=image8-Jul-27-2026-07-17-32-2545-AM.png" width="650" height="374" alt="seo tools teams need" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 650px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;SEO tools often have overlapping features, which makes it difficult to know which one (or ones) actually fit. The goal is a stack that covers all five of the below functions well, using as few tools as possible to keep costs down without sacrificing quality.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Keyword Research&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Keyword research is the starting point for almost every SEO initiative. It determines what content gets created and which &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/blog/tabid/6307/bid/6080/40-essential-seo-terms-marketers-should-know-glossary.aspx"&gt;SEO terms&lt;/a&gt; a page is optimized to rank for.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;SEO tools help with keyword research by surfacing search volume, competition data, and related terms that would otherwise take hours to find manually. &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/blog/tabid/6307/bid/22842/4-helpful-tools-for-identifying-the-right-keywords.aspx"&gt;Keyword research tools&lt;/a&gt; support topic clustering and intent analysis, enabling marketers to group related terms and understand whether searchers are looking to learn, compare, or buy. This matters because targeting the wrong intent, even for a high-volume keyword, often leads to content that ranks but doesn’t convert.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Technical Audits&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/technical-seo-guide"&gt;Technical SEO&lt;/a&gt; covers the behind-the-scenes factors that determine whether search engines can crawl, index, and properly understand a site. SEO tools help with technical SEO audits by scanning for issues like broken links, duplicate content, slow load times, and indexing errors that would be nearly impossible to catch manually across a large site.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Technical SEO tools detect crawl issues, Core Web Vitals problems, and schema errors. These issues matter because a technically broken site limits how much keyword research and content work can pay off.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Rank Tracking&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Rank tracking shows where pages stand in search results over time, which makes it possible to connect SEO work to actual outcomes rather than guesswork. SEO tools help with rank tracking by automatically monitoring keyword positions and flagging significant jumps or drops before they become bigger problems.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Rank tracking tools monitor keyword positions across locations and SERP features. A single average ranking doesn’t tell the entire story when results vary by city or device, or when they appear within features like local packs and featured snippets.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Featured Resource: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/how-to-find-serp-features-opportunities"&gt;How to Find SERP Feature Opportunities [+ Free Tools]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;On-page Optimization&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/blog/tabid/6307/bid/33655/a-step-by-step-guide-to-flawless-on-page-seo-free-template.aspx"&gt;On-page optimization&lt;/a&gt; covers everything on a page itself, including titles, headers, body content, internal links, and image alt text. SEO tools help with on-page content optimization by scoring content against ranking pages, suggesting related terms to include, and flagging missing elements like meta descriptions. On-page signals remain one of the clearest ways search engines determine relevance, and small fixes often produce outsized results compared to the effort involved.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Reporting&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Reporting turns SEO activity into evidence that the channel is working, which is often the difference between a program that keeps its budget and one that doesn’t. SEO tools help with &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/seo-report"&gt;SEO reporting&lt;/a&gt; by pulling traffic, ranking, and conversion data into dashboards that can be shared with stakeholders who don’t work in SEO daily. SEO results take time to materialize, and consistent reporting keeps leadership patient and engaged in the meantime.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;How We Evaluate SEO Tools for This Guide&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;ol start="1"&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Quality over quantity. &lt;/strong&gt;I looked at how well a tool does its core job rather than how many features it has. A keyword research tool that surfaces accurate volume and intent data beats one that bundles in extra features it only does adequately.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Accessibility for different budgets&lt;/strong&gt;. For every category, except platforms for scaling, I made sure there was at least one credible, free or low-cost option alongside the paid tools, since not every team can justify enterprise pricing on day one.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ease of use&lt;/strong&gt;. A tool with a steep learning curve might be powerful, but for each category, I prioritized at least one option that a beginner or a lean team could pick up quickly.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Connection to reporting and revenue&lt;/strong&gt;. SEO work that stays siloed in its own dashboard is harder to defend to stakeholde...

&lt;p&gt;AI search is no longer a niche experiment. According to &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/aeo/best-aeo-software"&gt;HubSpot’s own research&lt;/a&gt;, 42% of buyers now use AI search as part of their evaluation process — and it’s the top predictor of purchase intent. Brands that show up in those answers earn the conversation before a prospect even clicks.&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's AEO Tool" height="58" width="335" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;That’s exactly what Answer Engine Optimization (AEO) tools do. They track how your brand appears in AI-generated answers, identify gaps, and give you a clear path to fix them.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Two tools generating real buzz right now: HubSpot AEO and Rank Prompt. Both are purpose-built for AEO. Both track prompt-level visibility. But they serve different teams in different ways.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This guide breaks down the key differences so you can choose the right fit — and get started fast.&lt;/p&gt; 
